@startuml
skinparam dpi 150
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
hide empty members

' Question 1, parts 1-3
'
' 1. Nouns and candidate classes
'
' The nouns I found are library, members, items, title, catalogue number,
' books, three weeks, DVDs, one week, reference-only items, member number,
' hold, loan, librarians, catalogue, and late fee.
'
' I would keep Item, Member, Librarian, Catalogue, Hold, and Loan as classes.
' Hold needs to remember the member and item involved. Loan needs to remember
' who borrowed the item and when it is due. I would also add BorrowableItem
' and ReferenceOnlyItem to distinguish which items can be borrowed.
'
' The nouns I would not make into separate classes are:
' - Library: this refers to the whole system, so I do not need another class
'   for it unless I find a separate responsibility for it later.
' - Title: a String field in Item.
' - Catalogue number: a unique identifier field in Item.
' - Book and DVD: values of an ItemFormat enum. The only stated difference
'   in their behaviour is how long they can be borrowed for.
' - Three weeks and one week: loan-period values, or 21 and 7 days.
' - Member number: an identifier field in Member.
' - Late fee: an amount stored in Member. I would use a separate class if
'   the system needed to track individual charges or payment history.
'
' 2. Verb phrases and responsibilities
'
' - "borrow items" / "can be borrowed": Member requests the borrowing, and
'   BorrowableItem.borrow(member) checks availability and creates a Loan.
' - "has a title and a unique catalogue number": Item stores these fields.
'   Catalogue checks that a new item's catalogue number is not already used.
' - "borrowed for three weeks" / "borrowed for one week": BorrowableItem
'   provides the loan period, and Loan stores the resulting due date.
' - "cannot be borrowed": ReferenceOnlyItem does not provide a borrow method.
' - "log in with a member number": I would initially assign logIn to Member.
' - "place a hold": Member requests it, and BorrowableItem.placeHold(member)
'   checks that the item is on loan and adds a Hold to its collection of holds.
' - "is currently on loan": BorrowableItem keeps track of its current Loan.
' - "add new items to the catalogue": Librarian initiates this action, and
'   Catalogue.addItem(item) checks and stores the new item.
' - "waive a member's late fee": Librarian.waiveLateFee(member) asks Member
'   to clear its fee amount.
''
' 3. Inheritance and interfaces
'
' I would make Item abstract, with BorrowableItem and ReferenceOnlyItem as
' subclasses. They are both items and share a title and catalogue number.
' Item would also have a format field to say whether it is a book or DVD.
'
' I would use a Borrowable interface with borrow(member) and
' getLoanPeriodDays(). BorrowableItem implements it, but ReferenceOnlyItem
' does not. This means borrowing code can require a Borrowable, while the
' catalogue can work with any Item. The interface is useful for expressing
' that capability, although this small design could use the subclasses alone.
'
' I would not make Book and DVD subclasses just to give them different loan
' periods. For a borrowable item, its format determines whether the period
' is 21 or 7 days. Keeping format separate also lets a book or DVD be
' reference-only without needing more subclasses for each combination.
'
' I would keep Member and Librarian separate for now. They are both users,
' but the specification only gives members a member number and login
' behaviour. Being users alone does not give them shared code to inherit.
' If both later needed shared account details or login behaviour, I would
' consider an abstract LibraryUser superclass.

' Your task (Chapter 8, Question 1): draw the UML class diagram for the library
' specification — items, books, DVDs, reference-only items, members, librarians,
' and holds.
'
' Define each type as a box, then connect them with the right arrows. One box
' (Item) is filled in as an example — decide on the rest yourself. There is more
' than one defensible answer.
'
' PlantUML cheat-sheet:
'   interface Name { + method() : Type }
'   abstract class Name { # field : Type }
'   class Name { - field : Type }
'   Parent    <|-- Child    ' generalization: Child extends Parent
'   Interface <|.. Class    ' realization:    Class implements Interface
' (Putting the parent/interface on the LEFT of the arrow keeps it on top, which
'  is the convention used in these notes.)

abstract class Item {
  - title : String
  - catalogueNumber : String
  - format : ItemFormat
  + getTitle() : String
  + getCatalogueNumber() : String
  + getFormat() : ItemFormat
}

' TODO: add boxes for Book and DVD. What differs between them — a whole class,
'       or just the value of a loan-period field?

enum ItemFormat {
  BOOK
  DVD
}

' TODO: reference-only items cannot be borrowed at all. Decide how to say that
'       in the diagram: a subclass, or an interface (e.g. "Borrowable") that
'       only some items realize?

interface Borrowable {
  + borrow(member : Member) : Loan
  + getLoanPeriodDays() : int
}

class BorrowableItem {
  - /loanPeriodDays : int
  + borrow(member : Member) : Loan
  + getLoanPeriodDays() : int
  + isOnLoan() : boolean
  + placeHold(member : Member) : Hold
}

class ReferenceOnlyItem

note right of BorrowableItem
  loanPeriodDays is derived from format:
  BOOK = 21 days, DVD = 7 days.
  borrow requires no current loan.
  placeHold requires a current loan.
end note

' TODO: add Member and Librarian. What do they have in common? Add the common
'       supertype if you think one is warranted.

class Member {
  - memberNumber : String
  - lateFee : BigDecimal
  + logIn(memberNumber : String) : boolean
  + borrow(item : Borrowable) : Loan
  + placeHold(item : BorrowableItem) : Hold
  + clearLateFee() : void
}

class Librarian {
  + addItem(catalogue : Catalogue, item : Item) : void
  + waiveLateFee(member : Member) : void
}

class Catalogue {
  + addItem(item : Item) : void
  + findItem(catalogueNumber : String) : Item
}

note right of Catalogue
  addItem rejects a catalogue number
  that is already in use.
end note

' TODO: add whatever represents a hold, and connect it to the classes that need
'       to know about it.

class Hold {
  - placedOn : LocalDateTime
}

class Loan {
  - borrowedOn : LocalDate
  - dueDate : LocalDate
  + getDueDate() : LocalDate
}

' TODO: add the arrows between all of your types.

Item <|-- BorrowableItem
Item <|-- ReferenceOnlyItem
Borrowable <|.. BorrowableItem
Item ..> ItemFormat : uses

' These associations also represent the stored object references/collections.
Catalogue "1" --> "0..*" Item : items
BorrowableItem "1" -- "0..1" Loan : current loan
Loan "0..*" --> "1" Member : borrower
BorrowableItem "1" <--> "0..*" Hold : holds
Hold "0..*" --> "1" Member : requested by

Member ..> Borrowable : requests borrowing
Member ..> BorrowableItem : requests a hold
Librarian ..> Catalogue : adds items
Librarian ..> Member : waives late fee

@enduml
